## Trust and implementation limits

Synthetic fixtures exercise the actual algorithms and are explicitly host-authored, not real account/provider proof. Non-deserializable constructors separate Agent JSON from trusted Rust callers; they do not authenticate invented labels, receipts or host data. Actual caller must resolve current owner-approved acceptance policies (a partial Integrity/Data check is not automatically Goal/Task acceptance), consistent cumulative journals, pinned identities/paths and actual isolation covering shared Git metadata/subprocess writes. Host-grant enforcement is not claimed to be an OS filesystem/network sandbox.

ReadyDecision is a candidate batch tagged with expected plan and ledger revisions. It reserves nothing and starts nothing. Current unknown/cancelling effects, including older-plan root actions, conservatively block all new dispatch until actual reconciliation. Same workspace serializes even when marked read-only. Different roots need exact plan-bound host isolation and write-set proof, distinct pinned inode identities, no ambiguous case/Unicode root overlap and every shared quota. Unknown physical/alias cases serialize. OwnerApproval nodes wait for trusted owner results; neither Agent prose nor successful execution constitutes acceptance.
